# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines settings template
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   This file is a template for your settings. It holds no real passwords or
#   keys. To make your own settings file, copy this file to a file named
#   ".env" (with the dot at the start) in the same folder:
#
#     cp .env.example .env               (Linux, Mac, WSL or Git Bash)
#     Copy-Item .env.example .env        (Windows PowerShell)
#
#   Then open .env in a text editor and change the values that you need. For
#   the first start, you must change only POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
#
# HOW GU HEADLINES USES .env
#   Docker runs GU Headlines as four parts, called containers:
#     db       The database (PostgreSQL). It stores the stories.
#     web      The website that visitors see.
#     worker   The background program. It checks the news sites and the job
#              boards on a schedule.
#     caddy    The front door (Caddy, a free web server). It answers the
#              visitors on port 80 (http) and port 443 (https), and passes
#              each request on to web. It also gets the certificate for
#              https and renews it by itself.
#   When you start GU Headlines with "docker compose up", Docker Compose
#   reads .env. It gives each setting to the containers as an environment
#   variable (a named value that a program reads when it starts). The
#   programs read their settings only when they start. After you change
#   .env, apply the change with this command:
#
#     docker compose up -d
#
#   Docker restarts only the containers that need the new values.
#
# HOW TO WRITE A SETTING
#   - Write one setting on each line, in the form NAME=value.
#   - Do not put spaces before or after the "=".
#   - You do not need quotes, also for a value with spaces:
#       SITE_NAME=Guam News Now
#   - Lines that start with "#" are notes. Docker ignores them.
#   - Write a note on its own line. In a value, a space followed by "#"
#     starts a note, and Docker ignores the rest of the line.
#   - Do not use the "$" sign in a value. Docker Compose reads "$name" as
#     the name of a different variable and replaces it. For example, it
#     changes ab$cd to ab. If a value must contain "$", put the value in
#     single quotes:
#       POSTGRES_PASSWORD='ab$cd'
#   - For a yes/no setting, write true or false.
#   - To use the default of a setting, put "#" at the start of its line, or
#     delete the line. Do not leave a value empty, unless its note says that
#     empty is OK.
#
# KEEP YOUR .env PRIVATE
#   Your .env file holds secrets: the database password and the API keys.
#   (An API key is a password that a service gives to a program.) A person
#   who has a key can use that service as your site.
#   - Do not share .env. Do not show it in a screenshot or a chat message.
#   - Do not put real values in .env.example. That file is part of the code,
#     and everyone can read it.
#   - The .gitignore file tells Git to ignore .env, so "git commit" and
#     "git push" do not send it anywhere. The .dockerignore file keeps .env
#     out of the program image. Do not remove these rules.
#   - Keep a copy of .env with your backups. Without the database password,
#     GU Headlines cannot open its own database.
#   - If other persons learn a key, make a new key at the service. Put the
#     new key in .env, and delete the old key at the service.
#
# HOW TO READ THE NOTES BELOW
#   Required   "yes" means that GU Headlines does not start without it.
#   Default    The value that GU Headlines uses when the line is missing.
#   Example    A value in the correct form.
#   Secret     "yes" means: keep the value private (see above).
#   More       The guide in the docs folder that tells more.

# =============================================================================
# Database
# More: docs/settings.md#database
# =============================================================================
# The database stores the stories, the job postings and the results of each
# check. Docker makes the database on the first start, with the three values
# below. After that, the database keeps those first values, also when you
# change them in this file. Set these values once, before the first
# "docker compose up", and do not change them after that.

# POSTGRES_DB
#   The name of the database.
#   Required: no. Default: guheadlines. Leave it as it is.
POSTGRES_DB=guheadlines

# POSTGRES_USER
#   The user name that the website and the worker use to sign in to the
#   database.
#   Required: no. Default: guheadlines. Leave it as it is.
POSTGRES_USER=guheadlines

# POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   The password of that database user.
#   Required: yes. Without it, Docker Compose stops with the message
#   "set POSTGRES_PASSWORD in .env".
#   Secret: yes. The value below is only a placeholder that everyone can
#   read. Replace it before the first start.
#   How to make a strong password: run "openssl rand -hex 24" and copy what
#   it prints. It prints only letters and numbers, so it is safe here.
#   Example: 3f9c1e0b7a... (48 letters and numbers)
#   You do not type this password anywhere else. Keep a copy in a safe place.
#   Do not change the password after the first start. The database keeps its
#   first password, so a new value here locks GU Headlines out of the
#   database. To change it safely, follow docs/settings.md#database.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# =============================================================================
# Web address and https
# More: docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet
# =============================================================================
# Visitors do not talk to the website (web) directly. They talk to caddy,
# which passes each request on to web.
#
# Without a domain name, the site works with plain http:// on port 80. Open
# it at http://localhost on this computer, or at the IP address of the
# server from a different device, for example http://203.0.113.5.
#
# For https (the padlock in the browser), the site needs a domain name, such
# as guamheadlines.com. https needs a certificate, and caddy gets one free
# from Let's Encrypt, but only for a domain name, not for an IP address.
# caddy renews the certificate by itself, so you do not have to.
#
#   How to turn on https:
#     1. Buy a domain name from a domain registrar (a company that sells
#        domain names, for example Cloudflare, Porkbun or Namecheap). A .com
#        name costs about 10 to 15 US dollars a year.
#     2. In the DNS settings of your domain at the registrar, add an
#        "A record". (DNS is the address book of the internet. An A record
#        tells it the IP address of a name.)
#          Type     A
#          Name     @   (the "@" means the domain itself, guamheadlines.com)
#          Value    the IP address of your server. Your server company
#                   shows it in its control panel and in its welcome email.
#        For www.guamheadlines.com too, add a second A record with the name
#        www and the same IP address.
#        On Cloudflare, also set "Proxy status" to "DNS only" (a grey cloud)
#        for each record.
#        Do not add an "AAAA" record (an IPv6 address) unless you know that
#        IPv6 works on your server.
#     3. Wait until the name points at your server. This takes from a few
#        minutes to a few hours. To check it, run this on the server:
#          getent hosts guamheadlines.com
#        It must print the IP address of your server. If it prints nothing,
#        wait and try again.
#     4. Make sure that ports 80 and 443 of the server are open to the
#        internet (docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet tells how).
#     5. Put the domain name in SITE_DOMAIN below, and apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     6. Check it: wait one minute, then open https://guamheadlines.com (your
#        own domain). The browser must show the site with a padlock. If it
#        does not, run this command and look for lines with "error":
#          docker compose logs --tail 30 caddy

# SITE_DOMAIN
#   The domain name of the site, without https:// and without a "/" at the
#   end.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (plain http:// on port 80, no https).
#   Example: guamheadlines.com
#   For two names, put a comma between them. Then both names show the site
#   over https, and caddy gets a certificate for each name:
#     guamheadlines.com, www.guamheadlines.com
#   Each name must already point at this server (steps 2 and 3 above).
#   Until it does, caddy cannot get its certificate. It tries again by
#   itself, and the site does not open over https or http.
#   When SITE_URL (below) is empty, GU Headlines tells search engines that
#   https://<the first name> is the address of the site.
#   Secret: no.
SITE_DOMAIN=

# HTTP_PORT
#   The port for http:// on this computer. A port is a number that tells
#   the computer which program gets a request. When an address has no port,
#   the browser uses port 80 for http://, so with 80 the address is simply
#   http://localhost or http://<server address>.
#   Required: no. Default: 80.
#   On a server with a domain name, keep 80. Let's Encrypt checks the domain
#   on port 80 or 443 before it gives a certificate, and caddy uses port 80
#   to send http:// visitors on to https://.
#   Change it only on a computer where a different program already uses
#   port 80. Docker then stops with "port is already allocated" or "address
#   already in use". For example, with HTTP_PORT=8000, open the site at
#   http://localhost:8000.
#   Only Docker Compose reads this setting. Inside Docker, caddy always uses
#   port 80, and the website always uses port 8000.
HTTP_PORT=80

# HTTPS_PORT
#   The port for https:// on this computer. When an address has no port,
#   the browser uses port 443 for https://.
#   Required: no. Default: 443.
#   On a server with a domain name, keep 443. Change it only on a computer
#   that does not use https, where a different program already uses port
#   443 (Docker then stops with "port is already allocated").
#   Only Docker Compose reads this setting.
HTTPS_PORT=443

# =============================================================================
# Website
# More: docs/settings.md#website
# =============================================================================

# SITE_NAME
#   The name of the site. It shows in the top bar, in the browser tab and at
#   the bottom of each page.
#   Required: no. Default: GU Headlines.
#   If you change the name, also change the picture that shows when a person
#   shares a link to the site (see docs/settings.md#website).
SITE_NAME=GU Headlines

# SITE_TAGLINE
#   A short sentence about the site. It shows at the top and the bottom of
#   each page and in the browser tab of the home page. Search engines show it
#   at the start of the description of the site.
#   Required: no. Default: Guam news, updated every hour
#   If you change SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES, change this text to agree with it.
SITE_TAGLINE=Guam news, updated every hour

# PAGE_SIZE
#   How many stories a page shows before the "Older" link.
#   Required: no. Default: 30.
#   Use a number from 5 to 100. GU Headlines uses 5 for a smaller number and
#   100 for a larger number.
PAGE_SIZE=30

# =============================================================================
# Search engines
# More: docs/maintenance.md#search-engines
# =============================================================================
# Fill in these values only after the site is on the internet (see
# docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet). While the site runs only
# on your own network, leave them empty. Empty is OK for all three.

# SITE_URL
#   The public address of the site. GU Headlines gives this address to
#   search engines for each page. It goes in the canonical link (the "main
#   address" of a page), in the sitemap and in link previews. A search
#   engine then lists each page only one time, under one address.
#   Required: no. Default: empty. Then GU Headlines uses https:// and the
#   first name in SITE_DOMAIN. If SITE_DOMAIN is empty too, each page uses
#   the address that the visitor used.
#   With SITE_DOMAIN set, you need SITE_URL only to choose a different main
#   address, for example the www name:
#     https://www.guamheadlines.com
#   Example: https://news.example.com
#   The value must start with https:// or http://. If it does not, GU
#   Headlines ignores it. A "/" at the end is OK.
SITE_URL=

# GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION
#   A code that proves to Google Search Console that the site is yours.
#   Required: no. Default: empty.
#   Where to get it: in Google Search Console, add the site as a "URL prefix"
#   property and choose "HTML tag". Google shows a tag like this one:
#     <meta name="google-site-verification" content="AbC123xyz">
#   Copy only the text between the quotes after content=, here AbC123xyz.
#   Secret: no. Each visitor can see this code in the page source.
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# BING_SITE_VERIFICATION
#   The same type of code, for Bing Webmaster Tools. Choose its "HTML Meta
#   Tag" option, and copy only the text between the quotes after content=.
#   Required: no. Default: empty.
#   Secret: no.
BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# =============================================================================
# Advertising (Google AdSense)
# More: docs/settings.md#advertising
# =============================================================================
# With both values set, the home page, the Jobs page, the Sources page and
# the Obituaries page show one ad each, at the top of the right-hand column.
# With both values empty, the site shows no ad and loads no ad code. Empty is
# OK for both. To show the ad on another topic's page too, open
# config/categories.yaml, find the topic, and add the line "show_ad: true"
# under its name (lined up with "name:"), the same way Obituaries has it.
# The two values come from the ad code that AdSense gives you:
#   <ins class="adsbygoogle"
#        data-ad-client="ca-pub-1234567890123456"   <- ADSENSE_CLIENT
#        data-ad-slot="9876543210"                  <- ADSENSE_SLOT
# Copy only these two values. Do not paste the full ad code anywhere. The
# site writes the ad code itself, because its security rules block ad code
# that is pasted in.

# ADSENSE_CLIENT
#   Your AdSense publisher ID.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no ads).
#   Example: ca-pub-1234567890123456 (pub-1234567890123456 is also OK)
#   Where to find it: in AdSense, go to Account > Settings > Account
#   information. The "Publisher ID" line shows it. You can also paste the
#   line from the ads.txt page of AdSense, or its verification code. The
#   site takes the ID out of it.
#   With only this value, AdSense can verify the site, but the site shows no
#   ad. With this value, the site also serves the ads.txt file that AdSense
#   looks for. If the value has no publisher ID in it, the site acts as if
#   the value is empty, and AdSense says that it found no ads.txt file.
#   After you change this value, run "docker compose up -d". The automatic
#   update does not read a changed .env until the next update.
#   To check it: open https://your-domain/ads.txt in a browser (put your
#   own domain in). It must show one line, such as:
#     google.com, pub-1234567890123456, DIRECT, f08c47fec0942fa0
#   AdSense can take a few days to see a new ads.txt file.
#   Secret: no. It shows in the page source and in the ads.txt file of the
#   site.
ADSENSE_CLIENT=

# ADSENSE_SLOT
#   The number of your ad unit. To make an ad unit, go to Ads > By ad unit >
#   Display ads in AdSense.
#   Every page with an ad uses this same ad unit, so one number is enough.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no ads).
#   Example: 9876543210 (only digits)
#   Secret: no.
ADSENSE_SLOT=

# =============================================================================
# Visitor stats (private)
# More: docs/settings.md#visitor-stats
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines can count how many times each page is opened, and how many
# times readers follow a story's link to its news site (a "story click"). You
# see the totals on a private page of your site, /stats, after you type a
# password. It shows the top stories, the news sources and the topics that
# readers open most, and where readers come from: the website that each
# visit started from (such as google.com or facebook.com), or no website for
# a visit from a bookmark or the typed address. The counting happens on your
# own server:
#   - No script runs in the visitor's browser, so the pages stay as fast as
#     before. The PageSpeed scores do not change.
#   - No cookie is set, no outside service is used, no API key is needed,
#     and no IP address is stored. The site keeps only one number for each
#     page (or story, or website that sent a visit) for each day.
#   - Search engines, link previews and other robots are not counted.
# While the stats are on, each story link first opens a short address on
# your own site, such as https://guamheadlines.com/go/123. That address
# counts the click and sends the reader on to the story at once. Search
# engines are told not to follow these links.
# The disclaimer on the Sources page gets a short paragraph that tells
# visitors about the counts.
# Empty is OK: then nothing is counted, there is no /stats page, and the
# story links go straight to the news sites.
#
# How to turn the stats on:
#   1. Make a long random password. On the server (or in WSL), run:
#        openssl rand -hex 16
#      It shows 32 letters and digits, such as 3f9c2a7e5b1d4c8f9a0e6b2d7c4f1a3e
#      You can also make up a password of 16 or more letters and digits.
#   2. Put the password after STATS_PASSWORD= below, and save the file.
#   3. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   4. Open the stats page in a browser: your domain followed by /stats,
#      for example https://guamheadlines.com/stats
#      The browser asks for a user name and a password. Type any user name
#      (for example stats) and the password from step 1. The browser
#      remembers it until you close the browser.
#   The counts start at zero when you turn the stats on. A new view or click
#   can take up to a minute to show on the page.
# To turn the stats off, make the value empty again and run step 3. The
# counts stay in the database, and come back when you turn the stats on.

# STATS_PASSWORD
#   The password of the private visitor stats page (/stats). A password here
#   also turns the counting on.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no counting and no stats page).
#   Example: 3f9c2a7e5b1d4c8f9a0e6b2d7c4f1a3e
#   Where to get it: you make it yourself (step 1 above).
#   Use only letters and digits. A "$" sign, or a space followed by "#",
#   changes the value (see HOW TO WRITE A SETTING at the top of this file).
#   Secret: yes. A person who has it can see your visitor numbers. Open the
#   stats page only over https (with SITE_DOMAIN set). Over plain http, the
#   password travels without protection.
STATS_PASSWORD=

# =============================================================================
# Newsletter feed (for SendFox or another email service)
# More: docs/newsletter.md
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines makes a feed of its top stories for an email newsletter. A
# feed (RSS) is a list of stories in a format that programs can read. An
# email service such as SendFox reads the feed and makes an email from the
# stories for your subscribers. The feed is at your domain followed by
# /feed.xml, for example https://guamheadlines.com/feed.xml
#   - On the days and at the time below (Guam time), the worker picks the
#     top stories that it found since the time before: the stories that
#     readers clicked most first (this needs the visitor stats above, else
#     the newest come first), at most 3 from one news site. This set of
#     stories is an "edition".
#   - The feed then shows the same edition until the next one. So the email
#     service sees new stories only just after an edition is made. Make the
#     editions about two hours before the emails go out, so the email
#     service has time to read the feed.
#   - Each story in the feed has its headline, photo, news site and the
#     first lines of its intro (not the full story), and a link to the
#     story. While the visitor stats are on, the links pass through
#     /go/<number>, so clicks in the emails show on the stats page too.
#   - No page of the site links to the feed, and search engines are told
#     not to list it.
# How to connect SendFox, step by step: docs/newsletter.md
# The defaults below make an edition every Tuesday and Friday at 10:00 in
# the morning, for emails that go out at noon.

# NEWSLETTER_DAYS
#   The days of the week when the worker makes a new edition. Write the day
#   names in English, or their first three letters, with a comma between
#   them.
#   Required: no. Default: tue,fri
#   Example: mon,wed,fri
#   Empty: no feed (the /feed.xml address shows "Not found").
NEWSLETTER_DAYS=tue,fri

# NEWSLETTER_TIME
#   The time of day when the worker makes the edition, in Guam time (ChST).
#   Write it on a 24-hour clock: 10:00 is 10 in the morning, and 14:30 is
#   half past 2 in the afternoon. Make it about two hours before your email
#   service sends the email.
#   Required: no. Default: 10:00
NEWSLETTER_TIME=10:00

# NEWSLETTER_STORIES
#   The number of stories in each edition.
#   Required: no. Default: 12. Minimum: 1. Maximum: 50.
NEWSLETTER_STORIES=12

# =============================================================================
# Checking for news
# More: docs/settings.md#checking-for-news
# =============================================================================
# The worker visits each news site in config/sources.yaml on a schedule. It
# saves the new stories and their photos.

# SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker checks all the news sites, in minutes. The checks
#   follow the clock: 60 means at the start of each hour, 30 means at :00
#   and :30, and 120 means every second hour.
#   Required: no. Default: 60. Minimum: 5.
#   Use a number that divides an hour evenly (15, 20 or 30) or a number of
#   whole hours (60, 120 or 180). Checks more often than every 30 minutes
#   rarely find more news, and they send more requests to the news sites.
SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60

# SCRAPE_ON_START
#   true: check the news sites and the job boards immediately each time the
#   worker starts.
#   false: wait for the next scheduled check.
#   Required: no. Default: true.
SCRAPE_ON_START=true

# MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE
#   The maximum number of new stories that the worker reads from one site in
#   one check. A busy site, or the first check, can have more. The worker
#   gets the rest in catch-up checks (see CATCH_UP_SECONDS). This limit
#   prevents too many requests to one site at the same time.
#   Required: no. Default: 40. Minimum: 1.
MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE=40

# CATCH_UP_SECONDS
#   Sometimes the worker cannot read all of a site in one check. The site
#   had more than MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE new stories, or it answered "too many
#   requests". Then the worker comes back for only that site after this many
#   seconds. It comes back until the site is done, and then it goes back to
#   the regular schedule. If a site asks for a longer pause, the worker waits
#   that long, up to 10 minutes.
#   Required: no. Default: 60. Minimum: 30.
CATCH_UP_SECONDS=60

# JOBS_INTERVAL_HOURS
#   How often the worker reads the job boards for the Jobs page, in hours.
#   The list of job boards is in config/jobs.yaml. The worker reads them at
#   its first check after it starts, and then once in each period of this
#   many hours.
#   Required: no. Default: 6. Minimum: 1.
#   More: docs/jobs.md
JOBS_INTERVAL_HOURS=6

# EVENTS_INTERVAL_HOURS
#   How often the worker reads the event calendars for the Events page, in
#   hours. The list of calendars is in config/events.yaml. The worker reads
#   them at its first check after it starts, and then once in each period of
#   this many hours. Each check keeps the events of the next 90 days.
#   Required: no. Default: 3. Minimum: 1.
#   More: docs/events.md
EVENTS_INTERVAL_HOURS=3

# =============================================================================
# Weather, typhoon warnings and tides
# More: docs/settings.md#weather
# =============================================================================
# The worker also reads the weather and the tides for Guam from three
# services of the United States government. All are free. They need no key
# and no sign-up.
#   - The National Weather Service (NWS) office in Tiyan: the watches,
#     warnings and advisories for Guam and its coastal waters, the forecast
#     (for the week and for each hour), the latest reading at Guam
#     International Airport, the surf forecast (rip currents, surf height,
#     water temperature) and the coastal waters forecast (wind and seas, for
#     boating).
#   - The Joint Typhoon Warning Center (JTWC): the tropical storms and
#     typhoons in the western North Pacific.
#   - NOAA Tides & Currents: the times and heights of the high and low tides
#     (see TIDE_STATION below).
# The site shows the weather in three places:
#   - A banner at the top of every page while NWS has a watch or a warning
#     for Guam, for example a Typhoon Warning. A warning is red and a watch is
#     amber. A visitor can open the banner to read the full NWS text.
#   - A weather box at the top of the home page: the temperature, the
#     forecast, the storm list, today's high and low tides and the
#     advisories (for example a Small Craft Advisory).
#   - The Weather page (the "Weather" link in the top bar): all of the above
#     in more detail, the next 24 hours, the week, the surf and the sea, and
#     the tides for the day, the week or the month. Each part links to the
#     service that it comes from.
# If the worker cannot reach a service, the site keeps the last weather that
# it got. A warning stays in the banner until its end time.

# WEATHER_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker reads the weather, in minutes. A new warning shows
#   on the site at most this many minutes after NWS sends it, plus a few
#   minutes for the site and the browsers to show the new page.
#   0: do not read the weather. The site shows no banner, no weather box and
#   no Weather page.
#   Required: no. Default: 10. Minimum: 5 (or 0 to turn it off).
#   How to check that it works:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines weather
#   It reads the weather and the tides now and shows one line for each part
#   (alerts, conditions, forecast, hourly, surf, marine, storms and tides):
#   the data that it saved, or "ERROR" and the reason.
WEATHER_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10

# TIDE_STATION
#   Which NOAA tide station the tide times come from. A tide station is a
#   place where NOAA measures or works out the tides; each one has a number,
#   its "station ID". Tide times are a little different around the island,
#   so you could pick the station nearest to the beaches your visitors care
#   about. The default, 1630000, is Apra Harbor, which most Guam tide tables
#   use.
#   Tide times are worked out years ahead, so the worker reads the next month
#   only every 6 hours (and at once when you change this setting).
#   Empty (TIDE_STATION=): no tides on the site. The rest of the weather
#   stays.
#   Required: no. Default: 1630000 (Apra Harbor).
#   To find another station's ID:
#     1. Open https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/tide_predictions.html in a
#        web browser.
#     2. Find Guam on that page: in its list of states and territories, or
#        on its map.
#     3. Each station is shown with its name and its ID, a number like
#        1630000. Copy the ID.
#     4. Put it here, for example: TIDE_STATION=1630000
#     5. Restart the worker and the website (docker compose up -d), then
#        check it with: docker compose exec worker guheadlines weather
#        The "tides" line shows the station's name and the tides, or ERROR
#        and the reason (for example a wrong ID).
#   Anything other than letters and digits turns the tides off.
TIDE_STATION=1630000

# =============================================================================
# Power and water outages, and gas prices
# More: docs/settings.md#outages-and-gas-prices
# =============================================================================
# The Outages page (the "Outages" link in the top bar) lists the power and
# water outages that the island's utilities announce, by day (today, coming
# up, and the last 3 days), and the boil-water notices of the last 30 days.
# They come from three public pages. None needs a key or a sign-up:
#   - Power: the Guam Power Authority's (GPA) outage notices on GuamALERTS,
#     the island's official alert service (web.guamalerts.com), which GPA's
#     own website links to.
#   - Water: the Guam Waterworks Authority's (GWA) outage updates, the page
#     that GWA's website calls "Current Outages".
#   - Boil-water notices: from GWA, and from the Guam Environmental
#     Protection Agency (Guam EPA) news.
# Outages are only on that page: not in the warning banner and not on the
# home page. Each notice links to the agency's own notice. If the worker
# cannot reach a page, the site keeps the notices that it already has and
# says when it last reached that agency, with a link to its page.

# OUTAGES_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker reads the outage notices, in minutes. A new outage
#   shows on the site at most this many minutes after the agency posts it,
#   plus a few minutes for the site and the browsers to show the new page.
#   0: do not read them. The site has no Outages page and no "Outages" link.
#   Required: no. Default: 15. Minimum: 5 (or 0 to turn it off).
#   How to check that it works:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines outages
#   It reads the three pages now and shows, for each, how many notices it
#   found and the first few titles, or "ERROR" and the reason.
OUTAGES_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15

# GAS_PRICE_DAYS
#   The sidebar (the column beside the stories, on every page) shows Guam's
#   gas prices: regular, premium and diesel, for one gallon. Prices are the
#   same at every station on the island. No agency or company publishes them
#   in a form a program may read, so the site reads them from the local news
#   stories that it already collects ("Gas prices up again: $5.94 a gallon of
#   regular"). Each price shows the day of its story and links to it.
#   This setting is how many days back the site looks for such a story. A
#   price from an older story is not shown, because it may be out of date.
#   When no story in that time gives a price, the sidebar shows no gas
#   prices at all (nothing is made up).
#   Because the prices come from the news, they change on the site when the
#   news reports a change, usually the same day that the stations change
#   them.
#   0: no gas prices on the site.
#   Required: no. Default: 45. Maximum: 365.
GAS_PRICE_DAYS=45

# =============================================================================
# Sources that need a key
# =============================================================================
# Two sources need a free API key: the USAJOBS job board (Jobs page) and the
# Guam Legislature's calendar (Events page). (An API is a way for programs to
# get data from a service. The key tells the service which program asks.)
# Without its key, GU Headlines skips that source and shows the reason on the
# site. All other sources work without a key. Empty is OK for all three values.

# --- USAJOBS key (federal jobs on the Jobs page) ---
#   How to get the key:
#     1. Open https://developer.usajobs.gov/apirequest/ and fill in the
#        form. Use an email address that you can read.
#     2. Wait for the email from USAJOBS. It contains the key.
#     3. Put the key in USAJOBS_API_KEY below, and the same email address
#        in USAJOBS_EMAIL.
#     4. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     5. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines jobs --check --source usajobs
#        It must show a few USAJOBS postings. If it shows an error, copy the
#        key and the email address again.
#   More: docs/jobs.md#usajobs

# USAJOBS_API_KEY
#   The key for the USAJOBS job board (jobs with the United States federal
#   government).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (GU Headlines skips the USAJOBS board).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 and 2 above.
#   Secret: yes.
USAJOBS_API_KEY=

# USAJOBS_EMAIL
#   The email address that USAJOBS sent the key to. USAJOBS asks for this
#   address in each request. GU Headlines sends it only to USAJOBS. It does
#   not show it on the site.
#   Required: only together with USAJOBS_API_KEY. Default: empty.
#   Example: you@example.com
#   Secret: keep it private.
USAJOBS_EMAIL=

# --- Google Calendar key (the Legislature's calendar on the Events page) ---
#   The Guam Legislature keeps its calendar (sessions, public hearings,
#   committee meetings) in Google Calendar. GU Headlines reads it through
#   Google's Calendar API, which needs a key. The key is free: reading a
#   public calendar a few times a day stays far inside Google's free daily
#   limit, and Google does not ask for a credit card for this.
#   You need a Google account (a Gmail address works).
#   How to get the key:
#     1. Open https://console.cloud.google.com/ and sign in with your Google
#        account. If Google asks you to accept its terms, accept them.
#     2. Make a project (a folder for your settings at Google): click the
#        project list at the top of the page (it may say "Select a project"),
#        click "New project", type a name such as GU Headlines, and click
#        "Create". Wait a few seconds, then make sure the new project is
#        selected in the project list.
#     3. Turn on the Calendar API for that project: open
#        https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/calendar-json.googleapis.com
#        and click "Enable".
#     4. Make the key: open
#        https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#        click "Create credentials", then "API key". Google shows the new
#        key: a long line of letters and numbers that starts with "AIza".
#        Copy it.
#     5. Limit the key, so it is useless for anything else if someone sees
#        it: on the same page, click the key's name (or "Edit API key").
#        Under "API restrictions", choose "Restrict key", tick "Google
#        Calendar API" in the list, and click "Save".
#     6. Put the key in GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY below.
#     7. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     8. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines events --check --source legislature
#        It must show a few Legislature events. If it says "API key not
#        valid", copy the key again. If it says the API "has not been used in
#        project ... or it is disabled", do step 3 again and wait 5 minutes.
#   More: docs/events.md#google-calendar-key

# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY
#   The key for Google's Calendar API. GU Headlines uses it only to read
#   public calendars listed in config/events.yaml (google_calendar). It sends
#   the key only to Google, and never shows it on the site or in the logs.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (GU Headlines skips the Legislature's
#   calendar, but still lists the Legislature's session agendas).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 to 5 above.
#   Looks like: a line of 39 letters, numbers, dashes and underscores that
#   starts with AIza.
#   Secret: yes.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Being a good visitor to the news sites
# More: docs/settings.md#being-a-good-visitor
# =============================================================================

# PER_HOST_DELAY
#   The number of seconds to wait between two requests to the same site. A
#   larger number is gentler to the site. To slow down only one site, set
#   request_delay on that site in config/sources.yaml.
#   Required: no. Default: 1.5. A decimal number is OK.
PER_HOST_DELAY=1.5

# SCRAPER_WORKERS
#   How many sites the worker checks at the same time. The pause in
#   PER_HOST_DELAY still applies to each site.
#   Required: no. Default: 4. Minimum: 1.
SCRAPER_WORKERS=4

# REQUEST_TIMEOUT
#   The number of seconds to wait for a site to answer. After that time, the
#   worker stops that request and tries again at the next check.
#   Required: no. Default: 25.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT=25

# RESPECT_ROBOTS_TXT
#   true: obey the robots.txt file of each site. In that file, a site tells
#   which of its pages robots (programs like the worker) can read.
#   Required: no. Default: true. Keep it true.
#   To make an exception for one site, set ignore_robots: true on that site
#   in config/sources.yaml (see docs/news-sources.md#robotstxt).
RESPECT_ROBOTS_TXT=true

# SCRAPER_USER_AGENT
#   The name that the worker gives to the news sites and the job boards in
#   each request (the "User-Agent"). The owners of those sites see it in
#   their logs. Visitors to your site do not see it.
#   Required: no. Default: Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0)
#   You can add a way to reach you, so that a site owner can ask you a
#   question before they block you. The address of your site is sufficient.
#   Example: Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0; +https://news.example.com)
#   To use it, remove the "# " at the start of the line below, and put your
#   own text after the "=". USAJOBS gets a different name, which GU
#   Headlines makes from its settings above.
# SCRAPER_USER_AGENT=Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0; +https://news.example.com)

# =============================================================================
# Photos
# More: docs/settings.md#photos
# =============================================================================

# STORE_IMAGES
#   true: keep a copy of the main photo of each story on this computer, made
#   smaller and saved as WebP (a compact picture format). Old stories then
#   keep their photos after the news site deletes them.
#   false: load each photo from the news site each time a person views a
#   page.
#   Required: no. Default: true.
#   Docker keeps the copies in its "media" volume (a storage area that stays
#   when the containers restart). Each photo is saved twice: a copy for
#   phones (about 30 to 60 KB) and a small copy, 360 pixels wide, for
#   computers and tablets, where the photo is drawn smaller (about 10 to
#   20 KB). Each browser loads only the one it needs, so pages load faster.
STORE_IMAGES=true

# IMAGE_MAX_WIDTH
#   The width, in pixels, of the larger saved photo copies (the ones for
#   phones). 720 looks sharp on the site, also on phones. A larger number
#   makes larger files. A change applies only to photos that the worker
#   saves after the change. The small copies are always 360 pixels wide.
#   Required: no. Default: 720.
IMAGE_MAX_WIDTH=720

# IMAGE_STORAGE_GB
#   The most disk space the saved photos may use, in GB (gigabytes; 1 GB is
#   1,000 MB). The photos grow by roughly 2 to 4 GB a year. When they use
#   more than this, the worker deletes the photos of the oldest stories
#   until they use 90% of it, so your server's disk never fills up with
#   photos. Those old stories then show the photo from the news site
#   itself, for as long as the news site still has it. New stories always
#   get their photos saved.
#   0: no limit. Photos are kept forever (watch your disk space, see
#   docs/maintenance.md#disk-space).
#   Required: no. Default: 5. Decimals are fine, for example 2.5.
#   How to pick a number: look at how big your server's disk is (your
#   server provider's control panel shows it, or run "df -h /" on the
#   server and read the "Size" column of the first line). Leave at least
#   half of the disk for everything else. On a 20 GB disk, 5 is fine; on a
#   60 GB disk, you could use 20.
#   How to see how much space the photos use now:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines photos
#   It shows the space used and the limit, and does the tidy-up at once
#   (the worker also does it by itself after each news check).
IMAGE_STORAGE_GB=5

# =============================================================================
# Alerts in Discord
# More: docs/maintenance.md#alerts-in-discord
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines can post a message in a Discord channel when something needs
# your attention. Discord is a free chat app for phones and computers, and
# the Discord phone app shows each message as a notification. Alerts come:
#   - when a news site, job board or event calendar has not worked for a
#     day, and when it works again (the worker posts these),
#   - when a news site has gone quiet: it still answers, but has had no new
#     story for much longer than usual. Its feed or pages may have changed,
#     so the stories are no longer found. One more alert comes when it has
#     new stories again. (The worker posts these. There is nothing to set:
#     see docs/maintenance.md#source-health)
#   - when a new story mentions one of the words in ALERT_KEYWORDS (below),
#   - when the site stops answering, when the news has not been checked for
#     3 hours, and after each automatic update (scripts/auto-update.sh posts
#     these; see docs/maintenance.md#automatic-updates).
# Without DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL, no alerts are posted. Everything else works.
# While the visitor stats are on (STATS_PASSWORD), the stats page also shows
# how each source is doing, under "Source health" at the bottom.

# --- Discord webhook link ---
#   A webhook is a secret link that lets a program post messages in one
#   Discord channel. It can only post. It can't read your messages.
#   How to get the link (easiest on a computer, in the Discord app or at
#   https://discord.com/app in a web browser):
#     1. Sign in to Discord. If you have no account, make a free one at
#        https://discord.com/register
#     2. Make your own Discord server (a private group, not a computer):
#        in the column on the left, press the "+" button (Add a Server),
#        then "Create My Own", then "For me and my friends". Give it a
#        name, for example GU Headlines alerts, and press "Create". It gets
#        a channel named "general". You can use that channel.
#     3. Point at the channel name and press the gear button next to it
#        (Edit Channel). Open "Integrations", then "Webhooks", then press
#        "New Webhook".
#     4. Press the new webhook to open it. Change its name to GU Headlines
#        (the name shown on each alert). Press "Copy Webhook URL". Press
#        "Save Changes" if Discord shows that button.
#     5. Paste the link in DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL below. It looks like
#        https://discord.com/api/webhooks/1234567890123456789/AbCd...
#     6. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     7. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines alert-test
#        A test message shows in the channel within a few seconds. If you
#        see an error instead, copy the link again (step 4).
#     8. Get the alerts on your phone: install the Discord app on your
#        phone and sign in. Press and hold your server's icon, open
#        "Notifications" and choose "All Messages". Discord sends phone
#        notifications only while you are not using Discord on a computer.
#   More: docs/maintenance.md#alerts-in-discord

# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL
#   The webhook link of the Discord channel that gets the alerts.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no alerts).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 to 5 above.
#   Secret: yes. A person who has the link can post messages in your
#   channel. If other persons learn it, delete the webhook (step 3), make a
#   new one, and put the new link here.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ALERT_AFTER_HOURS
#   How many hours a news site, job board or event calendar must fail
#   before the alert comes. Sites have short problems now and then (a slow
#   server, a restart), and these fix themselves. A day (24) leaves time for
#   that. A site that fails for a day probably changed, or now turns
#   automated visitors away.
#   Required: no. Default: 24. Minimum: 1.
ALERT_AFTER_HOURS=24

# ALERT_KEYWORDS
#   Words that you want to hear about. When a new story's headline or intro
#   (its first lines) mentions one of these words, a message comes in your
#   Discord channel with the headline, the news site, the word found and the
#   link to the story. Each story comes only once. When several stories
#   come in the same check, they come together in one message.
#   This needs DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL (above).
#   How to write the words:
#     - Put a comma between them. A word can also be a phrase of several
#       words, such as power outage. Then those words must be next to each
#       other, in that order.
#     - Upper and lower case don't matter. Accents don't matter either:
#       Hagatna also finds Hagåtña.
#     - Only whole words are found, also with "s" or "es" at the end:
#       shooting also finds shootings, but fire doesn't find firefighters.
#     - Put a * at the end of a word to find every word that starts with
#       it: evacuat* finds evacuate, evacuated and evacuation.
#     - No quotes are needed. Don't use a "$" sign or a "#" (see HOW TO
#       WRITE A SETTING at the top of this file).
#   Stories hidden with "muted:" in config/sources.yaml are left out, like
#   on the site. Only news from the last 2 days counts, so when you add a
#   news site, its older stories don't come.
#   After a change, apply it:
#     docker compose up -d
#   To check: "docker compose exec worker guheadlines alert-test" sends a
#   test message that names your words.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no keyword alerts).
#   Example: typhoon, shooting, power outage, evacuat*
ALERT_KEYWORDS=

# =============================================================================
# Troubleshooting
# More: docs/settings.md#troubleshooting-output
# =============================================================================

# LOG_LEVEL
#   How much the website and the worker write to their log. To read the log
#   of the worker, run "docker compose logs worker". Use one of these values:
#     DEBUG     Each story that the worker saves or skips, and the reason.
#               Use it to find a problem with one site. It writes a lot.
#     INFO      The normal amount.
#     WARNING   Only problems.
#     ERROR     Only errors.
#   Required: no. Default: INFO.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# =============================================================================
# Running without Docker (advanced)
# More: docs/how-it-works.md#running-without-docker
# =============================================================================
# Without Docker, GU Headlines does not read this file. Set the same names as
# environment variables in your shell or in your service file instead. The
# names below are only for that case. Do not put them in .env when you use
# Docker, because they stop the containers from finding the database and
# the photos.
#   DATABASE_URL    The full address of the database, for example
#                   postgresql://guheadlines:the-password@localhost:5432/guheadlines
#                   With it, GU Headlines does not use POSTGRES_DB,
#                   POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
#   POSTGRES_HOST   The computer that runs the database. Default: localhost.
#   POSTGRES_PORT   The port of the database. Default: 5432.
#   CONFIG_DIR      The folder with sources.yaml, categories.yaml and
#                   jobs.yaml. Default: the config folder of the project.
#   MEDIA_DIR       The folder for the saved photos. Default: data/media in
#                   the project folder.
